window.docContents[119].resolve({"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#chapter-algebraic-graph-theory":{"contents":"section\nnamespace SimpleGraph\nvariable {V : Type*} {G : SimpleGraph V}\n\n\nAlgebraic graph theory studies a graph via algebraic objects attached to it, above\nall its automorphism group (Group (G ≃g G)), which measures how symmetric the\ngraph is, and its adjacency matrix (adjMatrix), whose powers count walks in the\ngraph. The two are related in an obvious way: a permutation of the vertices is an\nautomorphism iff it preserves the adjacency matrix.\n\nIn this chapter we start from Mathlib's group structure on the automorphisms of a\ngraph, and show that isomorphic graphs have isomorphic automorphism groups\n(Iso.autCongr). We show that the automorphism group of the infinite path pathNat\nis trivial (aut_pathNat_eq_one) and that of the cycle graph C[n], for n ≥ 3, is\nthe dihedral group (autCycleGraphMulEquivDihedral).\n\nNext we introduce the adjacency matrix. We show that every vertex of a\nvertex-transitive graph lies on the same number of closed walks of each length\n(IsVertexTransitive.card_walk_self_eq_trace_div), and that isomorphic graphs have\nthe same characteristic polynomial (Iso.charpoly_adjMatrix), although\nnon-isomorphic graphs can share one. We then show that eigenvalues (spectrum) of\nG give eigenvalues of its Mycielskian, scaled by the golden ratio\n(mem_spectrum_adjMatrix_mycielskian). We also survey Mathlib's results on the\nLaplacian (lapMatrix), whose kernel has dimension equal to the number of connected\ncomponents (card_connectedComponent_eq_finrank_ker_toLin'_lapMatrix), and on\nstrongly regular graphs (IsSRGWith) such as the Petersen graph (petersenGraph)\nand the Hoffman–Singleton graph (hoffmanSingletonGraph). To prepare for the later\nsections, we show that the Hamming cube model hypercubeHamming of the hypercube is\nisomorphic to our recursive box product construction Q[n] (hypercubeIsoHamming).\n\nThe final three sections are more advanced. Using the Hamming cube model of Q[n],\nwe prove that its automorphism group is the hyperoctahedral group ℤ/2 ≀ Sₙ, of\norder 2 ^ n * n! (autHypercubeMulEquivHyperoctahedral). We then count copies of\none graph in another. The unlabelled copies of K[s] are just the s-cliques, and\nin general the number of labelled copies is the number of unlabelled copies times the\nnumber of automorphisms (labelledCopyCount_eq_copyCount_mul_labelledCopyCount).\nFinally we show that every copy of Q[k] in Q[n] is an induced subcube, and count\nthese copies (labelledCopyCount_hypercube).\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean","header":"12. Algebraic Graph Theory","id":"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#chapter-algebraic-graph-theory"},"/Appendix/#section-chapter-8-walks":{"contents":"* pathNat.path: walk on the infinite path from i to i + j.* pathGraph.path: walk along the finite path graph P[n + 1] from 0 to\nFin.last n.* HypercubeVert.hammingPath: path between two hypercube vertices.* HypercubeVert.hammingDist: Hamming distance between two hypercube vertices\n(number of differing coordinates).* HypercubeVert.grayStart / HypercubeVert.grayEnd: start and end vertices of the\nGray code walk.* hypercube.grayPath: Gray code walk on Q[n].* Walk.mycielskianZigZag: zig-zag walk in mμ(G).* Walk.mycielskian: walk in mμ(G) constructed from a walk in G (protected, to\ncoexist with the graph constructor mycielskian).* Walk.mycielskianClosed: closed walk in mμ(G) constructed from a closed walk in\nG (a cycle if the original is one).* Walk.posaRotate: Pósa rotation of a path walk.* Walk.posaClosed: closing a Pósa path to obtain a cycle.* Walk.IsMaximalPath: a maximal path — a path that cannot be extended at either\nend, i.e. every neighbour of either endpoint already lies on it.* IsOrePair: Ore's condition at a pair of vertices — if distinct and non-adjacent,\ntheir degree sum is at least |V|.* IsClosed: a closed graph — one with no eligible pair, i.e. any two distinct\nvertices with degree sum at least |V| are adjacent.* closureStep: one round of the closure process — join every eligible pair at once.* closureAux: fuel-limited iteration of closureStep.* closure: the Bondy–Chvátal closure of a graph (computable).* IsOreGraph: an Ore graph — every pair of vertices satisfies Ore's condition\nIsOrePair.\n\nFrom the exercises:\n\n* chordedC5: C[5] with the two chords 0 — 2 and 0 — 3, a graph that is not\nan Ore graph but whose closure is complete.* Walk.IsMaximumPath: a maximum-length path — a path such that no path in G is\nlonger.\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Appendix\u0009Definitions","header":"Chapter 8: Walks","id":"/Appendix/#section-chapter-8-walks"}});